  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nDiminishing cloud cover across the CWA in wake of the weekend's\nstorm system that brought much needed and ample precipitation,\nespecially east of the Divide. IR shows warming cloud tops as\nthe aforementioned system makes its way eastward across the\nupper Great Plains to the Great Lakes stretching southward all\nthe way to the Gulf of America. WV shows the main finger of the\nPFJ well to the north across Canada pushing cyclonically around\nan upper level low over the Gulf of Alaska. Another weak low\nnear California will be the next system to bring in storm\nactivity for Tuesday afternoon and evening with its slow\nprogression east and northeastward. \n\nWith the clear skies west of the Divide, expect fog development\nover some of the area. Jackson hole looks to be the best chance\nfor a heavier impact with the surrounding lakes and recent\nrainfall. Points further east of the Divide, the cloud cover\nlooks to hinder much of the area but possible (10-20%) around\nthe Casper area but confidence low at this point in time\ndependent on cloud cover by Monday morning sunrise. Beyond that,\nexpect light winds and seasonable temperatures into the\nafternoon with tranquil weather conditions to be had heading\ninto the next system for later Tuesday. \n\nThe aforementioned closed low near California will become more \nof an open and progressive wave with its northeastward movement \nthrough the upper Rockies Tuesday afternoon and evening. \nScattered rain showers and isolated thunderstorms likely \n(40-70%) for much of the area west of the Divide, with lower \nconfidence to the east (20-40%). The strongest PVA anomalies \nlook to occur across Sweetwater County through the upper Green \nRiver Basin for the strongest storms to occur. The likely \nimpacts will be that of heavy rainfall and gusty outflow winds.\nHi-res models are pushing the 36 hour limit on its output but \nit tends to be close enough at this point in time. The Winds \nwill likely block much impact to the east, but strong winds will\nbe the main factor pushing down into the Wind River Basin \ntowards sunset Tuesday evening. \n\nBeyond Tuesday's storm and shower activity, expect induce\nridging to build in through the Rockies that will bring above\naverage temperatures for the remainder of the work week. Winds\nwill be relatively tame for the week as well with no indications\nof fire weather concerns at this point in time. The next system\nlooks to push in by Friday night into the weekend as the\naforementioned Gulf of Alaska low extends down into the Pacific\nNorthwest as the PFJ dips into the continental US. Early \nindications of model runs keep the main impacts north into \nMontana but still affects to be had in the CWA with \nprecipitation and more seasonable temperatures for the weekend \nand into the following week.",
